@startuml component_deactivation

title Component Deactivation

box "Main loop thread" #F0F4FA
participant "ProcessGroupManager" as PGM
participant "Graph" as Graph
participant "Transition" as Transition
end box

queue "Worker Queue" as WorkerQueue
queue "Component Event Queue" as EventQueue

box "Worker thread" #FAF6F0
participant "IComponentController" as Monitor
participant "IComponent" as Component
end box

participant "Alive Supervision" as Supervision

box "OsHandler thread" #F4F0FA
participant "OsHandler" as OsHandler
participant "SafeProcessMap" as Map
end box

participant "Operating System" as OS

note over Graph: graph is in state kInTransition

' --- dispatch ----------------------------------------------------------------
activate Graph
Graph -> Transition ++: get components ready to be stopped
Transition --> Graph --: component is ready to be stopped
note over Graph
  A component is only ready once every component that
  depends on it has been stopped, so a transition stops
  the components in the reverse of their start order.
end note
Graph -> WorkerQueue: deactivation of the component
deactivate Graph

' --- execution ---------------------------------------------------------------
WorkerQueue -> Monitor ++: deactivation is taken by a free worker
Monitor -> Component ++: deactivate

opt component is supervised
    Component -> Supervision ++: stop supervising the process
    Supervision --> Component --:
    note right of Component
      Supervision is stopped before the process is asked to
      terminate, so the shutdown is not reported as a
      supervision failure.
    end note
end

Component -> OS: Send SIGTERM signal
Component -> Component: wait for the process to exit\nuntil the shutdown timeout expires

alt #F2FAF2 the process exits within the shutdown timeout
    OS -> OsHandler ++: child process has terminated
    OsHandler -> Map ++: look up the component for the process id
    Map --> Component: the process has exited
    deactivate Map
    deactivate OsHandler

else #Pink the shutdown timeout expires
    Component -> OS: Send SIGKILL signal
    OS -> OsHandler ++: child process has terminated
    OsHandler -> Map ++: look up the component for the process id
    Map --> Component: the process has exited
    deactivate Map
    deactivate OsHandler
end

' --- result ------------------------------------------------------------------
Component --> Monitor: deactivation complete
note over Monitor
  A deactivation has no failure outcome: the component
  escalates until the process is gone.
end note
Monitor -> EventQueue: component deactivated
deactivate Component
deactivate Monitor

' --- completion --------------------------------------------------------------
EventQueue -> PGM ++: component event
PGM -> Graph ++: apply the component event

alt the graph is still in transition
    Graph -> Transition ++: component has finished
    Transition --> Graph --: next components ready to be stopped
    note over Graph
      Once everything that has to be stopped is gone, the
      transition moves on to starting the components of the
      target Run Target - see Component Activation.
    end note

else the graph has left the transition meanwhile
    note over Graph
      The result is still consumed. Once the last component
      in flight has reported back, the graph settles in
      kUndefinedState.
    end note
end

deactivate Graph
deactivate PGM

legend bottom
  The two queues are the only interaction between the threads: the graph never
  touches a component directly and a worker never touches the graph. All graph
  and transition state stays confined to the main loop thread.

  Several components may be in flight at the same time; a single one is shown
  here for clarity.
end legend

@enduml
